n secretary said Monday evening.Hundreds of flights were canceled, including more than 600 into and out of Newark Liberty International Airport, as the outage hit every major airport in the New York City area.Transportation Secretary Sean Duffyalso said in an online post that “it will take some time to clear all existing delays” despite the repairs.The primary communications line at theFederal Aviation Administrationfacility in Philadelphia had failed earlier Monday, while the backup fiber optic line was inadvertently severed by railroad construction workers. The failures left controllers without radar or communications for hours.The outage brought commercial air traffic to a near-standstill at Newark for much of the day, with no commercial flights landing at the airp...", "published_at": "2026-09-22T13:26:30+00:00", "image_url": null, "source": {"name": "Fast Company", "tier": "independent", "url": "https://www.fastcompany.com"}, "bias_scores": [{"political_lean": 35, "sensationalism": 10, "opinion_fact": 25, "factual_rigor": 50, "framing": 17, "confidence": 0.286, "rationale": {"framing": {"connotation_score": 18.7, "keyword_emphasis_score": 0.0, "omission_score": 40.0, "headline_body_divergence": 25.8, "has_cluster_context": true}}}]}}, {"article": {"id": "82d9e106-e30d-4461-9a49-a18344cc5a9f", "title": "Trump official begs for cash as he shifts blame for flight chaos", "url": "https://www.rawstory.com/sean-duffy-2677914629/", "summary": "Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy took to Fox News Tuesday to shift blame for Monday’s mass flight disruptions onto Congress, which he blamed for not adequately funding his agency.Thousands of flights were cancelled or delayed Monday aftertechnical issuesrattled airports along the East Coast. The disruptions were reported to have been caused by a mistakenly cut fiber optic cable for a backup communications system that air traffic controllers attempted to switch to after a communications circuit that “manages flight traffic” fa